  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nBy Tuesday night, the cold front will stall out across northern \nMissouri and central Illinois and act as the focal point for \nadditional rounds of showers and storm to redevelop mid- to \nlate-week. A nearly stationary longwave trough over the western\nCONUS and ridging over the Southeast will keep our upper-level \nflow out of the southwest through Thursday as weak shortwaves \nare pulled northeastward into the Midwest. This will keep PoPs \naround Tuesday night into Thursday as scattered showers and \nstorms fire along and north of the stationary front. With Pwats \nover 1.75 inches likely (70-90%) area-wide through Thursday, the\nflooding threat will remain elevated, especially after the \nheavy rainfall tonight and Tuesday. Per the latest NBM, there is\na medium to high chance (50-70%) of total rainfall of 3 inches \nor more over much of eastern Iowa through Thursday with \nlocalized areas exceeding 5 inches possible (5-15%). Rises on \narea creeks and streams are likely.\n\nHeading into Friday, a Front Range low in Colorado is forecast to \nform due to forcing from a lobe of mid-level vorticity pulled \nnorthward from the subtropics. This will introduce a more southerly \ncomponent to the low-level flow as the stationary front converts to \na warm front and moves northward into Iowa. The upper-level flow is \nexpected to become zonal and force the Front Range low eastward \nFriday night into Saturday. The increased surface convergence will \nsupport another round of storms before the same boundary is left \nbehind for Sunday, keeping PoPs in the area.",
